Origin of Cybernetics (etymology not related to electronics at all) :
Steering a ship (Greek: kybernao / kybernetes) → steering or governing a state (Latin: gubernare / gubernator) → 
  science of civil government (1834 French: cybernétique) → 
  self-regulating feedback and control systems (1948: cybernetics) → anything computer, networked, or digital (1980s–present: cyber-).
Rooted in the Ancient Greek kybernetes ("steersman" or "pilot," from kybernao, "to steer or guide")—a term Plato used metaphorically for the art of steering a 
  state and which passed into Latin as gubernare to give English govern and governor—the word resurfaced in 1834 when French physicist André-Marie Ampère 
  coined cybernétique to classify the science of civil government, and in 1843 when Polish philosopher Bronisław Trentowski independently used kibernetika 
  for the art of political leadership, before MIT mathematician Norbert Wiener revived it in 1948 in Cybernetics: Or Control and Communication in the Animal 
  and the Machine to unite the study of self-regulating feedback loops and control systems across biology and engineering, after which its clipped prefix 
  cyber- spun off into 1960's cyborg, got popularized as cyberspace and cyberpunk by 1980s science fiction, and ultimately detached from systems theory 
  altogether during the internet boom to become a catch-all label for everything digital, networked, and electronic.

















